This PR changed `catch` to `catch (e)`, but `e` is never referenced in the catch body. The original form `catch {` (with no binding) was correct and cleaner. Notably, the same file already uses bare `catch` correctly in `fileExists` (line 220) and `applyTempFixForEventDescriptions` (line 267)—revert this change for consistency:

```ts
} catch {
  return undefined;
}
```

On Vite 6.3+, the outer `filter: { id: /\/vite-inject-mocker-entry\.js/ }` already ensures `source` matches before `handler` is called, making the inner `if (source === ENTRY_PATH)` check redundant. However, on Vite < 6.3 the `filter` property is ignored (the `handler` is still called for all IDs via Rollup's `{ handler }` object support), so this inner guard is necessary for backward compatibility. The same applies to all other inner `if (!filter(id))` guards across this PR. Without an explanatory comment, this code looks like dead code to future maintainers and may be incorrectly removed. Add a short comment:

```ts
// Inner check is required for Vite < 6.3 backward compat; the outer
// filter object is silently ignored by older Vite (rollup) versions.
if (source === ENTRY_PATH) {
```
